    "updated_at": "2026-08-31T15:47:54Z",
    "body": "I'll not open another issue for that yet, but more unclear is how this impacts initial `about:blank`.\n\n| | 0 | 1 | _2_ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| top | /a | | /b |\n| frames[0] | /i-a | about:blank | |\n\nIf the iframe is statically declared with a `src` that [is processed as about:blank](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#process-the-iframe-attributes) and we populate it with an `about:blank` entry, the load event comes synchronously in Chrome. I.e. its treated as an initial `about:blank`. It's async in Firefox.\n```\ndata:text/html,<iframe id=i onload=\"console.log('foo')\"></iframe><button onclick=\"i.src = 'https://example.com'\">click</button><button onclick=\"i.src = 'about:blank'\">click2</button><button onclick=\"location = 'https://example.org'\">away</button><script>console.log(\"bar\")</script>\n```\n\nIf the iframe is statically declared with a `src` that isn't processed as `about:blank` and we populate it with an `about:blank` entry, the load isn't synchronously in Chrome.\n```\ndata:text/html,<iframe id=i src='https://example.com' onload=\"console.log('foo')\"></iframe><button onclick=\"i.src = 'about:blank'\">click</button><button onclick=\"location = 'https://example.org'\">away</button><script>console.log(\"bar\")</script>\n```\n\n",
